      items: [
        { title: 'Assuming “no permit” means “no rules”', text: 'Zoning, setbacks, easements, HOA and deed rules can still apply.' },
        { title: 'Checking the city but not the HOA', text: 'Private documents may still control size, location, color, roof and siding.' },
        { title: 'Choosing the spot before checking setbacks', text: 'The most attractive location may not be allowed.' },
        { title: 'Adding utilities without approval planning', text: 'Electric, HVAC, plumbing or a finished interior can change permit requirements.' },
        { title: 'Assuming every county works the same', text: 'Expectations change across short distances in the Tri-State.' },
        { title: 'Waiting until the last minute', text: 'HOA meetings, review cycles and inspections can affect the schedule.' },
      ],
    },
    {
      type: 'cards', id: 'gather', title: 'What to Gather Before Contacting Any Office or HOA', nav: 'What to Gather',
